kind: perf
upstream: earcut.js (github.com/mapbox/earcut) PR #212, merged 2026-10-06 as
  2c222c7, not yet ported to earcut.hpp (github.com/mapbox/earcut.hpp, whose
  master matched our pin on 2026-11-01). Drop this once the port lands.

earcut.js dropped vertex skipping in its PR #195 and earcut.hpp followed: each
ear now continues from `next`, so every ear of a convex run fans from one
vertex, the triangles grow long and each hashed point check scans O(n) points,
which is quadratic. earcut.js declined to restore skipping (its issue #211) and
fixed it in #212 instead: a strictly convex ring has no vertex that could block
an ear, so check convexity once and skip the point checks until a candidate
fails. Every vertex of a strictly convex ring is an ear, so the cut order and
the triangles are exactly pristine's. A verbatim port of #212.

Measured 2026-11-01 in the trimkit-dev image (g++ 14.2 -O3, i7-12700K, one
pinned core), best of 7, pristine 177bd66 vs this patch alone:
  regular 40,000-gon              683 ms  ->   2.1 ms
  regular 100,000-gon            5067 ms  ->  15.4 ms
  comb, 2,500 teeth               0.7 ms  ->   1.0 ms  (sub-millisecond noise)
  rings with holes                unchanged: a ring with holes is never convex
Correctness: identical triangles to pristine on 2,150 random and 34 degenerate
polygons (differential run, 2026-11-01).
Covered by: tests/test_earcut.py::test_degenerate_rings (a 100,000-gon under a
time bound), fuzz/earcut.cpp.

